---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — Mila Business Solutions LLC"
  description="How Mila Business Solutions works: engineering principles, what we take on, and what we turn down."
>
  <Section narrow>
    <div class="reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1 style="margin-top:var(--space-3);">How we work</h1>
      <p class="lead" style="margin-top:var(--space-4);">
        Mila Business Solutions is a small consultancy built on two decades of
        enterprise data systems — the kind where an outage means the business
        stops, and a bad schema decision is still causing pain eight years
        later.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:var(--space-4);">
        That background shapes what we build. Most of what gets sold as AI
        automation is a thin layer over data that was never structured to
        support it. It demos beautifully and falls over in month three. We would
        rather fix the foundation first and tell you that plainly, even when
        it's the less exciting answer.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section sunken title="Principles we don't bend on">
    <div class="grid grid--2">
      <div class="principle">
        <h3>Uncertainty gets escalated, never guessed</h3>
        <p class="muted">
          An automated system that invents a plausible answer is worse than one
          that stops and asks. Every pipeline we build has an explicit "I don't
          know" path that reaches a human.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="principle">
        <h3>Nothing is deleted, only marked</h3>
        <p class="muted">
          User-generated data gets soft-deleted. Recovering a row someone
          removed by accident should be a query, not a restore from backup.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="principle">
        <h3>It has to survive a bad network</h3>
        <p class="muted">
          Connections drop, phones sleep, VPNs reconnect. Anything we build
          assumes the unreliable case is normal, because for anyone working
          remotely it is.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="principle">
        <h3>Idempotent by default</h3>
        <p class="muted">
          Jobs can be re-run. Installers can be applied twice. A retry should
          never be the thing that causes the incident.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="principle">
        <h3>Least privilege, even internally</h3>
        <p class="muted">
          Private networks get treated as hostile. Secrets stay server-side,
          credentials are scoped to one job, and "it's only on the internal
          network" is not a security model.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="principle">
        <h3>Handover is part of the work</h3>
        <p class="muted">
          If your team can't operate it without us, we haven't finished. The
          goal is a system you own, not a dependency on us.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section narrow title="What we turn down">
    <p>
      Being useful means being clear about the edges. We'll say no to:
    </p>
    <ul class="turn-down">
      <li>
        <strong>AI added because it should have AI.</strong> If a scheduled query
        and a well-built form solve it, that's what we'll recommend — and it will
        cost you less.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Automating a broken process.</strong> Making a bad workflow run
        faster just produces bad results sooner. The process gets fixed first or
        not at all.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Anything requiring a live production database credential we
        don't need.</strong> If read-only access to a replica will do, that's what
        we'll ask for.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Work where we'd be the wrong choice.</strong> Front-end design
        systems, mobile apps, and infrastructure at hyperscale are all real
        disciplines that aren't ours. We'll say so.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p style="margin-top:var(--space-7);">
      Still sounds like a fit? <a href="/contact/">Describe what's not working</a>
      — or <a href="/demo/">try the SQL review</a> first and judge the output
      before you spend an email on us.
    </p>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .principle h3 {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
  }

  .principle p {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  .turn-down {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: var(--space-5) 0 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
  }

  .turn-down li {
    padding-left: var(--space-5);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--c-border-strong);
    color: var(--c-fg-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  .turn-down strong {
    color: var(--c-fg);
  }
</style>
